        <p>The 24% bracket starts at $105,700.00 of taxable income, which is $121,800.00 of gross. Straight time stops $1,160.00 short of that line. An overtime hour pays $87.00, so 13.33 overtime hours a year close the gap, which is about 15 minutes a week. Thirteen hours leave you $29.00 short of the line, and the fourteenth hour crosses it, costing $13.92. That is the whole story of this table: crossing the bracket costs almost nothing at first, and the cost grows with the hours. At half an hour a week the 24% slice is $1,102.00 and costs $264.48. At one hour a week it is $3,364.00 and costs $807.36. At five hours a week it is $21,460.00 and costs $5,150.40. The point is not the cost. It is that the "22% bracket" label stops describing your year the moment you work any real overtime at this rate, and every overtime hour after the crossing keeps $59.46 instead of $61.20. Our <a href="/articles/which-states-have-daily-overtime/">daily-overtime guide</a> maps the states where a second threshold applies on top of the weekly one.</p>
