        <p>That take-home number comes with limits, and they matter. It is federal tax only, for a single filer taking the $16,100 standard deduction. No state tax is in it, and no 401(k) or health premium comes off it. It also assumes 40 paid hours in all 52 weeks, which no real year quite delivers. The detail most guides skip is in Step 2: $54,140 of the $104,540 taxable income actually sits in the 22% bracket, and that slice costs $11,910.80. Tax the whole year at a flat 22% and the federal bill comes out $5,288.00 too high. The raise section below puts a price on what that slice means for your next $1. The sections that follow run the same $58.00 rate through every schedule, every overtime pattern, and every unpaid-week gap, so you can find the version that matches your real year.</p>
