        <p>Read the last column from the top down and the pattern is the point of this section: fewer hours means more kept from each hour. At 20 hours a week you keep $48.70 an hour. At 40 hours you keep $45.05. The gap is $3.65 an hour on the same $58.00 rate, and the bracket is behind it. A 20-hour year carries $44,220.00 of taxable income, and none of it reaches the 22% bracket. A 25-hour year carries $59,300.00, and $8,900.00 of it lands in the 22% bracket, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up at all. Three rows matter more than the headline. First, the 35-hour week crosses $100,000 gross on its own, at $105,560.00, and its $83,091.46 of take-home already beats a $100,000 salary by $3,911.46, before overtime or a raise. Second, the 37.5-hour paid week, common in offices that pay for a 7.5-hour day, is $113,100.00 gross, which still beats a $110,000 salary by $3,100.00 gross and $2,180.85 of take-home. A 2.5-hour shorter week costs $7,540.00 gross and $5,304.39 of take-home. Third, the step from 30 to 40 hours is worth $30,160.00 gross and $21,217.56 of take-home ($1,768.13 a month). That is the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Part-time keeps a bigger share: at 20 hours you keep 84.0% of gross, against 77.7% full-time, and a 25-hour week at $58 beats a $70,000 salary's take-home by $3,798.90, while a 20-hour week beats a full-time $27-an-hour job by $3,342.56. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
